#OMNISIM R2025a utf8

# FOLDING A T-SHIRT WITH A FLOATING GRIPPER -- TWO SOLVERS, ONE WORLD.
#
# This is the demo the deformable work has been building toward, and the reason
# it is interesting is not the fold: it is that the fold requires MuJoCo and VBD
# to run SIMULTANEOUSLY and feel each other.
#
#   newtonSolver "mujoco+vbd"
#     the `mjc` entry owns ALL rigid bodies and ALL joints  (the gantry, the
#     table, the jaws)
#     the `vbd` entry owns ALL particles                    (the shirt)
#     a Proxy mapping couples them, so the gripper feels the fabric WITHOUT
#     MuJoCo ever knowing that particles exist.
#
# Both solvers must declare their FULL sets -- an unowned body or an unowned
# particle is not an error, it is an invisible dead patch -- which is why the
# world declares one solver string rather than per-node backends.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IS HARD HERE, so nobody mistakes a partial result for a broken one:
# folding a garment properly is a research problem, not a demo. ONE clean fold --
# pinch a sleeve edge, lift, carry it across the body, set it down -- is the
# deliverable. A full folding routine is not.
#
# ⚠ THE SELF-CONTACT TENSION, real and NOT resolved -- this demo runs it OFF.
# A fold STACKS fabric on fabric, so with self-contact off the carried sleeve
# sinks through the body and the fold is a ghost. But WorldInfo.wrl records the
# opposite requirement for the other half of the demo: GRASPING needs it OFF,
# measured at 24x on tracking error, because fabric gathered between the pads
# pushes ITSELF out of the jaws. The two halves of "pick up a shirt and fold it"
# want opposite values of one field, and this world currently runs the grasp
# value (OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT=0) because without a grip there is nothing
# to fold. The field below says 1; the environment overrides it.

EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/backgrounds/protos/OmniSimSky.proto"
EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSun.proto"
EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSunMarker.proto"

WorldInfo {
  gravity 9.81
  basicTimeStep 8
  coordinateSystem "ENU"
  # !! THE COUPLED PAIR, AND IT NOW HOLDS -- but it needs the environment below.
  #
  # ROOT CAUSE, for anyone who finds this world exploding again. OmniSim gives a
  # weld-pinned STATIC body a nominal mass of 1.0 kg. Under the coupled solver
  # the FIXED joint that pins it lives in the `mjc` entry and is DISABLED in the
  # `vbd` view, so inside the VBD solve the table was a FREE 1 kg body carrying a
  # 0.2 kg shirt through 56,362 penalty springs. It launched the garment off the
  # table and the solve went to NaN, every time. On whole-world "vbd" the same
  # nominal mass exists but the pinning joint is live in the same solver, which
  # is the entire difference between the two paths.
  #
  # It is compounded by an OPEN UPSTREAM BUG, newton-physics/newton#3795
  # (2026-08-04, unfixed): the VBD proxy wrench harvest ignores the per-body
  # soft-contact buffer cap, so the cloth is pushed by at most N contacts while
  # the rigid body is pushed back by ALL of them -- silent momentum injection.
  # Our cap was 2048 against 56,362 records needed (V+F+E of the mesh), and the
  # engine's own newton_solver.log had been printing that overflow all along.
  #
  # ⚠ THIS WORLD DOES NOT SIMULATE CORRECTLY FROM THE FILE ALONE. There are no
  # WorldInfo fields for the proxy knobs, so the fix lives in the environment and
  # the world cannot state its own physics:
  #
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_PROXY_MASS_SCALE=10000     <- the load-bearing one
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_RIGID_PARTICLE_BUFFER=65536
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_SOFT_KE=150000             }  from newton's own
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_SOFT_KD=0.0001             }  example_proxy_joint_gripper.py
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_VBD_ITERATIONS=5
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT=1             <- ON. See below; the 24x is dead.
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT_RADIUS=0.003
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT_MARGIN=0.0045
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_FULL_SURFACE_CONTACT=0     <- 8x, and free (see below)
  #   OMNISIM_CLOTH_ATTACH_CMD=<path>.jsonl    <- THE CARRY MECHANISM (below)
  #
  # !! THE CARRY IS A PARTICLE ATTACH, NOT A FRICTION PINCH. The campaign that
  # closed this world's "keeping the pinch through the LIFT" question ran 12
  # laptop configurations and 8 cloud seeds with valid negative controls, and
  # its best run -- a genuine 5.5 mm pinch -- still shed the fabric during
  # lift. The mechanism is structural: VBD contact friction is velocity-
  # regularised (friction_epsilon), so under a constant tangential load W the
  # contact CREEPS at u* ~ W*eps_u/(2*mu*N) -- nonzero for every mu (upstream
  # newton-physics/newton#3943; effort limits: #3944). So the fold's carry is
  # the same mechanism a mocap pin uses: OMNISIM_CLOTH_ATTACH_CMD names a
  # command file the controller (which inherits the engine env) appends
  # {"op":"attach","body":6,"point":[x,y,z],"radius":r} / {"op":"detach"}
  # lines to; the runtime pins the selected particles to the pad body and
  # acks the MEASURED selection count in <path>.ack. The friction pinch is
  # kept -- it shapes the fabric and the negative control exercises it -- but
  # the attach is what survives the traverse. Pinned by
  # tests/test_newton_cloth_attach.py; controller side: FOLD_ATTACH[_BODY/
  # _RADIUS/_MAX] in vbd_tshirt_fold.py.
  #
  # !! SELF-CONTACT IS ON, AND THE OLD "GRASPING NEEDS IT OFF (24x)" IS NOT A LAW
  # OF PHYSICS -- it was a GAP-ARITHMETIC BUG, now understood exactly. newton's
  # self-contact pushes two fabric surfaces apart until they sit
  # particle_self_contact_radius apart (a surface distance; the barrier is
  # evaluate_self_contact_force_norm in newton's particle_vbd_kernels.py). So a
  # two-layer pinch physically needs face-to-face >= 2*particleRadius + r_sc
  # before compression even starts. The old grasp worlds commanded 6.2 mm onto a
  # stack that needs 13.8 -- the fabric could only comply by EJECTING ITSELF,
  # and that ejection is the whole 24x. The fold controller now derives its gap
  # with the self-contact term (FOLD_SELF_CONTACT_RADIUS), and with it ON this
  # world both LOOKS right (no self-intersecting ghost -- the top view stops
  # being a black patchwork of the underside showing through) and holds a
  # measured 22 mm pinch at close. Cost at this resolution: 12.0 -> 21.8 ms/step
  # (1.8x), nothing like the 17x it costs at 37k particles.
  #
  # ⚠ WHAT IS STILL OPEN: keeping the pinch through the LIFT. Three failure
  # modes, all measured on this world, all with the aware gap:
  #   mu 1 (default)  clean 22 mm pinch at close, extruded during lift
  #   mu 2            jaws PRIED OPEN at close (131 mm excess): SOFT_MU is
  #                   GLOBAL, so sticky pads also mean a sticky TABLE, and
  #                   gathering fabric across a sticky table pries the jaws
  #   mu 1000         newton's own cloth-lift value: instant NaN here
  # The missing capability is PER-SHAPE particle friction (sticky pads, normal
  # table). That is an engine feature, not a tuning value.
  #
  # !! COST, MEASURED ON THIS WORLD (60 timed steps after a 20-step warm-up):
  #
  #   full-surface ON, iterations 10   187.98 ms/step   <- what it used to cost
  #   full-surface OFF                  23.62           8.0x
  #   + iterations 5                    14.88          12.6x
  #   + substeps 2                      12.00          15.7x
  #   ...whole-world "vbd", no coupling 11.86          15.9x
  #   ...+ CLOTH_CPU_MJ=1              106.70           7.2x SLOWER -- a trap
  #
  # Three things those rows say. Edge/face contact records are the ENTIRE cost:
  # one record per vertex, per edge AND per triangle, ~6x the vertex count, each
  # a penalty spring evaluated every substep of every iteration. Turning them off
  # changed the grasp signature by exactly zero here, because the pads are wide
  # against this mesh's 19.6 mm spacing -- re-check that if the pads ever get
  # thinner than the vertex spacing, which is the tunnelling case the flag exists
  # for. The COUPLING is cheap: 12.00 coupled vs 11.86 uncoupled is 1.2%, so
  # running two solvers is not what makes this world expensive. And
  # OMNISIM_NEWTON_CLOTH_CPU_MJ=1, which the engine's own log offers, is 7x
  # WORSE -- GPU utilisation drops to 37% as the per-substep host copies make it
  # CPU-bound.
  #
  # ⚠ NONE OF THIS MAKES THE MACHINE COOL, and that is a separate fact. Real time
  # needs <= 8 ms/step; this is 12.00, so the engine never idles and pins the GPU
  # for as long as it runs. A cheaper step just means more steps per second at
  # the same 100% utilisation. For watching rather than measuring: settle, then
  # PAUSE.
  #
  # Forgetting them does not fail loudly; it explodes, which is at least honest.
  # Promoting these to WorldInfo fields is the obvious follow-up.
  #
  # ⚠ SCOPE. SolverCoupledProxy is ONE MONTH OLD and upstream-labelled
  # experimental, with "proxy stability is tuning-sensitive" as its FIRST
  # documented limitation. No upstream example grasps a garment above 900
  # particles; newton's own garment demo uses ONE-WAY coupling and runs in
  # centimetres "for better numerical behavior of the VBD solver". Treat this
  # world as a controlled experiment, not a stable platform.
  # !! SINGLE-SWEEP "vbd" AGAIN -- and this time from measurement, not retreat.
  # The coupled pair works (it holds the cloth, the gantry drives, nothing NaNs)
  # but under a PINCH it oscillates: worst gantry tracking error measured
  # 880 mm on the coupled path (the on-screen "gripper glitch"; under-relaxation
  # 0.7 -> 324 mm, doc recipe 0.5/iter4 -> 525 mm, substeps 4 -> no better)
  # against 27 mm -- thirty-three times better -- with everything in ONE
  # SolverVBD sweep. WorldInfo.wrl predicts exactly this: across a proxy the
  # cloth only ever sees a jaw synced from the PREVIOUS rigid solve, and a
  # pinch resolved against a one-step-stale pad is a feedback loop. The price
  # (documented there): no mj_model, so raycast devices, welds, TouchSensor and
  # contact readback are gone, and Motor.maxForce is decorative. This demo uses
  # none of them, and joint POSITION readback works since the eval_ik fix.
  newtonSolver "vbd"
  # !! 2, MEASURED. Substeps are the second-biggest cost lever here and the one
  # with a hard floor: 4 -> 14.88 ms/step, 2 -> 12.00, 1 -> 6.07 and REAL TIME
  # (rtf 1.32) -- but 1 is UNSTABLE. It survived a 60-step benchmark and then
  # went NaN partway through the full run, ending at cz -2690 m. A perf probe
  # cannot certify stability; 2 is verified over the whole schedule (213/213
  # finite telemetry rows, garment at rest z 0.7571).
  newtonSubsteps 2
  newtonStatics TRUE
  # A pinch on fabric is a friction grasp, and the default friction will not hold
  # one. This is the field that sets contact friction -- WorldInfo.contactProperties
  # / coulombFriction was the ODE-path declaration and is NOT read.
  newtonGroundMu -1
  # !! NO newtonCone/newtonImpratio HERE, and that is a measured decision that
  # CONTRADICTS docs/guide/friction-grasp.md -- because that guide is about
  # grasping a RIGID part, and this world's contact is mostly soft.
  # Measured on this world, drop-to-table, everything else equal:
  #   newtonImpratio 10  -> shirt launched to z = 14.3 m off the table
  #   newtonImpratio 1   -> launched to 1.9 m, still fell through
  # Boosting the friction constraint to normal-constraint stiffness is exactly
  # what a 9,468-particle soft body cannot absorb. If a future grasp needs more
  # friction, raise newtonGroundMu, not the cone/impratio.
  newtonClothSelfContact 1
}

Viewpoint {
  # Three-quarter view from the near-left, low enough to read the fold as a
  # change in silhouette rather than as a flat pattern from above.
  position 1.05 -1.15 1.42
  orientation 0.055 0.183 0.981 2.132
}

OmniSimSky {
  luminosity 3.0
}
DEF SUN OmniSimSun {
}
DEF SUN_MARKER OmniSimSunMarker {
}

DEF FLOOR Solid {
  translation 0 0 0
  children [
    Shape {
      appearance PBRAppearance {
        baseColor 0.40 0.39 0.37
        roughness 1
        metalness 0
      }
      geometry Box {
        size 8 8 0.02
      }
    }
  ]
  name "floor"
  boundingObject Box {
    size 8 8 0.02
  }
}

# TABLE. Top face at z = 0.75 exactly -- the controller's descend target and the
# shirt's rest height are both built on that number, so if you move it, move them.
DEF TABLE Solid {
  translation 0 0 0.725
  children [
    Shape {
      appearance PBRAppearance {
        baseColor 0.46 0.34 0.22
        roughness 0.65
        metalness 0
      }
      geometry Box {
        size 1.4 1.0 0.05
      }
    }
  ]
  name "table"
  boundingObject Box {
    size 1.4 1.0 0.05
  }
}

# THE SHIRT.
#
# ⚠ ORIENTATION IS BAKED INTO THE MESH, NOT DECLARED HERE, and getting this
# wrong is silent. The garment is authored STANDING UP (local x sleeve-to-sleeve,
# local y front-to-back through the tube, local z hem-to-shoulder), so it has to
# be rotated -90 deg about X to lie flat. tshirt_lo_flat.obj carries that
# rotation already; the node declares none.
#
# The trap, paid for once: swapping the mesh for a coarser one WITHOUT swapping
# to its flat variant leaves a shirt standing upright, which collapses into a
# heap on contact. It still settles, still reports finite telemetry and still
# PASSes -- but the settled fabric ends up 300 mm from where the controller
# reaches, so the jaws close on air and every "grip" number is servo settling.
# If the grasp reads identical across wildly different cloth parameters, check
# the settled bbox against the grab point BEFORE tuning anything else.

DEF TSHIRT Cloth {
  # ⚠ THE -0.3217 IS NOT COSMETIC CENTRING. tshirt_md.obj's local z runs
  # 0 -> 0.6434, NOT +/-0.3217: the mesh origin is at the HEM, not the middle.
  # The rotation carries that offset straight through, so an untranslated shirt
  # lies entirely in +Y -- 143 mm of it overhanging a table that ends at y=0.50,
  # and, worse, the whole garment sitting where a symmetric grab point finds
  # zero vertices. Measured: a 70x100 mm pad footprint at (-0.28, 0.00) contains
  # NO fabric, so the demo and its own negative control would have been the same
  # experiment.
  #
  # z 0.90 rather than 0.86 for a second measured reason: at 0.86 the garment's
  # lowest vertices start at 0.7234, i.e. 27 mm INSIDE a table top at 0.75. With
  # self-contact ON that is the worst possible initial condition. 0.90 clears it.
  translation 0 -0.3217 0.90
  url [
    "meshes/tshirt_lo_flat.obj"
  ]
  density 0.3
  # Half the 9.37 mm mean edge. particleRadius is a collision THICKNESS: at or
  # above the vertex spacing every particle's sphere swallows its neighbours and
  # the sheet stiffens into cardboard.
  particleRadius 0.0046
  fixTop FALSE
  # !! NO STIFFNESS OVERRIDES -- ENGINE-DERIVED DEFAULTS, MEASURED.
  # Newton's own coupled-solver example uses tri_ke 1e5 / tri_kd 1e-2*tri_ke /
  # edge_ke 0.01 / particle_radius 0.01, and copying those numbers onto the
  # COUPLED path did not rescue it -- still NaN. They are tuned for a
  # 30x30 GRID that is PINNED at two edges and never touches anything, and they
  # do not transfer to a 9,468-vertex mesh RESTING on a table.
  # What measurably settles this garment is the engine's derived defaults with
  # particleRadius at half the 9.37 mm mean edge: rest at centroid z 0.7551,
  # min z 0.7470 against a table top at 0.750, stable for 300+ steps.
  diffuseColor 0.30 0.45 0.85
  appearance PBRAppearance {
    baseColor 0.24 0.37 0.72
    baseColorMap ImageTexture {
      url [
        "textures/woven_cotton_basecolor.png"
      ]
    }
    normalMap ImageTexture {
      url [
        "textures/woven_cotton_normal.png"
      ]
    }
    roughnessMap ImageTexture {
      url [
        "textures/woven_cotton_roughness.png"
      ]
    }
    occlusionMap ImageTexture {
      url [
        "textures/woven_cotton_occlusion.png"
      ]
    }
    metalness 0
    normalMapFactor 0.5
    IBLStrength 1.5
    sheen 0.55
    sheenColor 0.85 0.87 0.92
    sheenRoughness 0.32
    diffuseWrap 0.35
    transmissionColor 0.16 0.20 0.30
    transmissionPower 10
    transmissionThickness 0.45
    specularAntiAliasing 0.18
    textureTransform TextureTransform {
      scale 14 14
    }
  }
}

# THE FLOATING GRIPPER.
#
# "Floating" is three prismatic joints, not magic: a Robot pinned in space whose
# X/Y/Z sliders position a carriage, carrying two opposed jaw sliders. It is the
# same shape as the sponge world's gantry and it exists because a real arm would
# add IK and reach failures to a demo that is about CLOTH.
#
# ⚠ The jaws are POSITION_VELOCITY servos. `setForce` does NOT put a Newton joint
# into force mode -- the PD servo stays live and anchored at the last setPosition
# -- so a grip is made by commanding a position with a known INTERFERENCE inside
# the fabric's particle shell, not by asking for a squeeze force.
DEF GANTRY Robot {
  translation 0 0 1.35
  children [
    DEF GX SliderJoint {
      jointParameters JointParameters {
        axis 1 0 0
      }
      device [
        LinearMotor {
          name "gantry_x"
          maxForce 5000
          maxVelocity 5
          minPosition -0.60
          maxPosition 0.60
        }
        PositionSensor {
          name "gantry_x_sensor"
        }
      ]
      endPoint DEF BASE_X Solid {
        children [
          DEF GY SliderJoint {
            jointParameters JointParameters {
              axis 0 1 0
            }
            device [
              LinearMotor {
                name "gantry_y"
                maxForce 5000
                maxVelocity 5
                minPosition -0.45
                maxPosition 0.45
              }
              PositionSensor {
                name "gantry_y_sensor"
              }
            ]
            endPoint DEF BASE_Y Solid {
              children [
                DEF GZ SliderJoint {
                  jointParameters JointParameters {
                    axis 0 0 1
                  }
                  device [
                    LinearMotor {
                      name "gantry_z"
                      maxForce 5000
                      maxVelocity 5
                      minPosition -0.62
                      maxPosition 0.05
                    }
                    PositionSensor {
                      name "gantry_z_sensor"
                    }
                  ]
                  endPoint DEF CARRIAGE Solid {
                    children [
                      DEF JL SliderJoint {
                        jointParameters JointParameters {
                          axis 0 1 0
                        }
                        device [
                          LinearMotor {
                            name "jaw_left"
                            # !! 300, not 2000. These are POSITION_VELOCITY servos
                            # with targetKe = effortLimit * 10, so maxForce 2000
                            # gives kp = 20 kN/m and a 3 mm bite into fabric is
                            # ~60 N -- that does not grip cloth, it EXTRUDES it.
                            # Measured with the old value: grip excess peaked at
                            # 13.15 mm then decayed to 0.00 before the lift even
                            # finished. 300 gives kp = 3 kN/m, ~9 N over the same
                            # bite: a pinch, not a press.
                            maxForce 300
                            maxVelocity 5
                            minPosition -0.20
                            maxPosition 0.06
                          }
                          PositionSensor {
                            name "jaw_left_sensor"
                          }
                        ]
                        endPoint DEF LEFT_PAD Solid {
                          translation 0 -0.05 0
                          children [
                            Shape {
                              appearance PBRAppearance {
                                baseColor 0.70 0.72 0.76
                                roughness 0.35
                                metalness 0.2
                              }
                              geometry Box {
                                size 0.07 0.01 0.03
                              }
                            }
                          ]
                          name "left_pad"
                          boundingObject Box {
                            size 0.07 0.01 0.03
                          }
                          physics Physics {
                            density -1
                            mass 0.02
                          }
                        }
                      }
                      DEF JR SliderJoint {
                        jointParameters JointParameters {
                          axis 0 -1 0
                        }
                        device [
                          LinearMotor {
                            name "jaw_right"
                            # Mirror of jaw_left -- see the note there.
                            maxForce 300
                            maxVelocity 5
                            minPosition -0.20
                            maxPosition 0.06
                          }
                          PositionSensor {
                            name "jaw_right_sensor"
                          }
                        ]
                        endPoint DEF RIGHT_PAD Solid {
                          translation 0 0.05 0
                          children [
                            Shape {
                              appearance PBRAppearance {
                                baseColor 0.70 0.72 0.76
                                roughness 0.35
                                metalness 0.2
                              }
                              geometry Box {
                                size 0.07 0.01 0.03
                              }
                            }
                          ]
                          name "right_pad"
                          boundingObject Box {
                            size 0.07 0.01 0.03
                          }
                          physics Physics {
                            density -1
                            mass 0.02
                          }
                        }
                      }
                      Shape {
                        appearance PBRAppearance {
                          baseColor 0.55 0.57 0.60
                          roughness 0.4
                          metalness 0.3
                        }
                        geometry Box {
                          size 0.05 0.14 0.02
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                    name "carriage"
                    # ⚠ EXPLICIT COLLIDER, NOT OPTIONAL. A Solid with `physics`
                    # and no `boundingObject` does not get "no collision" here --
                    # the engine substitutes a SPHERE OF RADIUS 0.12 and warns.
                    # The carriage descends to the table, so that invisible 24 cm
                    # ball would barge into the shirt and the table top before the
                    # jaws ever reached the fabric, and the demo would fail in a
                    # way that looks like a cloth bug.
                    boundingObject Box {
                      size 0.05 0.14 0.02
                    }
                    physics Physics {
                      density -1
                      mass 0.4
                    }
                  }
                }
              ]
              name "base_y"
              # Same sphere-fallback trap as the carriage. These two links are
              # structural and never leave z ~ 1.35, well clear of the table, but
              # a 0.12 m default sphere on each is still a lie about the scene.
              boundingObject Box {
                size 0.04 0.04 0.04
              }
              physics Physics {
                density -1
                mass 0.6
              }
            }
          }
        ]
        name "base_x"
        boundingObject Box {
          size 0.04 0.04 0.04
        }
        physics Physics {
          density -1
          mass 0.8
        }
      }
    }
  ]
  name "gantry"
  controller "vbd_tshirt_fold"
}
